1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,600
Take a large number.

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:04,550
For example, ten thousand.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,900
What is the chance that it is prime?

4
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:09,050
Well, you probably answered 0.

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,800
I asked a silly question,
so I got a silly answer.

6
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:14,750
Ten thousand is not prime.

7
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:16,700
So let me try again.

8
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:20,050
Take a random number around ten thousand:

9
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,400
what is the chance that it is prime?

10
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:25,150
Well, that's not well-posed either.

11
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,500
What does "around" mean?
And what does "random" mean?

12
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:30,850
So let's define it more precisely.

13
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,800
We draw a number that
differs from ten thousand

14
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:36,950
by at most one percent of ten thousand,

15
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:39,900
and we do it so that all these numbers

16
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:42,650
are equally likely to be chosen.

17
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,000
We ask: what is the
probability that it is prime?

18
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,600
We have a computer, so this
is not a hard question.

19
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:55,350
We just need to count how many
primes there are in this range

20
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:58,100
and divide by the size of the range.

21
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:00,650
Well, we got an answer.

22
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,400
This wasn't a very
informative question either...

23
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:08,550
We asked about a specific number
and got a specific answer.

24
00:01:10,150 --> 00:01:13,550
Let's ask this question for many numbers.

25
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:16,700
Here too, it's not hard
to do with a computer.

26
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:19,250
We run over all the numbers in this range,

27
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:24,800
and for each one we compute the share
of primes within one percent of it.

28
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:30,850
We get a graph of the chance that
a number within one percent of n

29
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,400
is prime, as a function of n.

30
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:36,550
Let's call this function p₁%(n).

31
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:39,900
We don't expect an exact formula for it,

32
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:42,450
just as we don't expect

33
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,400
an informative formula for the primes.

34
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:49,950
But we can try to
understand how it behaves.

35
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:54,150
We see in the graph
that it is quite small.

36
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:57,700
It's a bit hard to see
things about small numbers.

37
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:01,250
So instead we'll talk about 1/p₁%(n).

38
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,400
Let's call this function r₁%(n),

39
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:09,250
the "rarity" of the primes around n.

40
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:13,400
We see that this function
increases, but rather slowly.

41
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,200
Let's switch to a logarithmic scale.

42
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,250
We see a clear pattern here:

43
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:24,800
to a very good approximation, it is
a linear function of the logarithm.

44
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:26,750
Let's check which one.

45
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,500
Again, a computer readily shows

46
00:02:29,650 --> 00:02:34,250
that the logarithm to base e
approximates the function r₁%(n) well,

47
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:37,600
at least in the range
where we can compute.

48
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:42,400
The choice of one percent
was rather arbitrary.

49
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:46,150
We can choose any other percentage
and get a similar result.

50
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,800
A 5% window

51
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:51,450
A 20% window

52
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:54,850
This looks great on the graph,

53
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,800
but how do we know this
is a real phenomenon

54
00:02:58,950 --> 00:03:02,550
that goes on to infinity, not
something that only looks right

55
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,500
in the range we checked?

56
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:06,850
How do we prove it?

57
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,200
And come to think of it, what on
earth do we need to prove here?

58
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:14,350
Well, the last question
is fairly easy to answer.

59
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:18,500
We want to compare our
function with ln(n).

60
00:03:18,650 --> 00:03:22,850
One way to do this is to
divide one by the other,

61
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,600
and we want to say that this
ratio is very close to 1.

62
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:32,550
Of course, when n is small, our
statistics are not very good yet,

63
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,500
and there can be a deviation.

64
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:37,850
But as n grows, the
deviation tends to vanish.

65
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,600
That's what limits were invented for.

66
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:44,150
So our claim is really
that the limit is 1.

67
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,300
And as we said, any other
percentage works too.

68
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:53,300
Gauss and Legendre conjectured
this, in a slightly different form,

69
00:03:55,350 --> 00:03:58,350
and Hadamard and de la
Vallée Poussin proved it.

70
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:01,500
It is called the Prime Number Theorem,

71
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:04,050
and it is the subject of this video.

72
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:08,600
Before the full proof, Chebyshev
proved a weaker version,

73
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:11,350
bounding the ratio from above and below

74
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:13,500
but the limit is not found,

75
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:16,650
and it fails for percentages
that are too small.

76
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:21,400
Later, Riemann developed a revolutionary
strategy for such problems.

77
00:04:23,650 --> 00:04:28,250
It was this strategy that eventually
led to the solution of the problem

78
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:30,800
and to much fascinating mathematics.

79
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:34,200
We won't prove the conjecture now.

80
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:38,350
Nor will we tell you about
Riemann's revolutionary ideas.

81
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:41,500
We won't even explain Chebyshev's proof,

82
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:43,450
although it isn't complicated.

83
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,200
Instead, we'll prove a theorem of Mertens

84
00:04:46,350 --> 00:04:48,350
which doesn't imply the conjecture,

85
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:52,300
but certainly makes a convincing
case that it should be true.

86
00:04:55,550 --> 00:04:58,550
Let's look at the number n!

87
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:00,700
and try to factor it.

88
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:03,650
How many times does 2 appear in it?

89
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:05,600
That's fairly easy:

90
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:08,350
once for every even number up to n,

91
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:11,900
once more for every number divisible by 4,

92
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:13,850
and so on.

93
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:16,500
What about 3? It's similar,

94
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:19,250
and so on for all the primes up to n.

95
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:21,600
Writing it this way is cumbersome,

96
00:05:21,750 --> 00:05:24,350
so we'll use product and sum notation.

97
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:29,150
These are quite large
numbers, so let's take logs.

98
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:31,700
The log of a product is a sum of logs.

99
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:34,650
We also know how to
take the log of a power.

100
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:38,800
These integer parts are rather
annoying, so let's drop them.

101
00:05:38,950 --> 00:05:40,750
That will give us an error term,

102
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,300
so the equality is no longer exact.

103
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:46,650
Now we can factor out n.

104
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:49,000
We are left with geometric series.

105
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:52,350
You surely remember the
geometric series formula.

106
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:56,100
We got n times the series of ln p/(p−1).

107
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:59,650
Nothing will happen if
we replace p−1 with p.

108
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:01,800
We're approximating anyway.

109
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:05,200
What about the other side?

110
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:08,750
A factorial is a product,
so again its log is a sum.

111
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:11,500
This sum is not so easy to compute,

112
00:06:11,650 --> 00:06:13,650
but we can approximate it well.

113
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:18,200
The point is that for 99%
of the numbers up to n

114
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:20,150
the log is roughly log n.

115
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:25,200
So, quite accurately, we
can replace it with n ln(n).

116
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:27,350
Now we can cancel n.

117
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:33,100
We got an inexact formula for the
sum of ln p/p over the primes.

118
00:06:33,250 --> 00:06:37,050
And if we work hard enough,
we can bound its error.

119
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:39,000
This is Mertens' theorem.

120
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:44,400
But how is this related to
the Prime Number Theorem?

121
00:06:44,550 --> 00:06:46,750
Let's go back to the inexact form.

122
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:48,700
There's an ln(n) here.

123
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:51,850
You surely know another series like this.

124
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:53,800
It's the harmonic series.

125
00:06:53,950 --> 00:06:55,750
So what did we get?

126
00:06:55,900 --> 00:06:58,300
So summing over all natural numbers

127
00:06:58,450 --> 00:07:01,250
is like summing over the primes only,

128
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:04,600
but giving each prime p the weight ln(p).

129
00:07:04,750 --> 00:07:07,950
It's just as if the primes around p

130
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:11,300
were spread ln(p) times more
sparsely than the integers,

131
00:07:11,450 --> 00:07:14,250
and so they have to be
counted with more weight

132
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:16,600
to balance their contribution.

133
00:07:16,750 --> 00:07:20,750
But this is exactly what the
Prime Number Theorem said.

134
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:25,100
Well, this doesn't really
prove the Prime Number Theorem,

135
00:07:25,250 --> 00:07:27,650
since it only tells us about averages.

136
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:32,200
There could be regions with
significantly fewer primes

137
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:34,550
and regions with significantly more.

138
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:36,700
But it gives us a sense of it.

139
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:40,850
In fact, one can deduce from
it the following weak version

140
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,400
of the Prime Number Theorem:

141
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,600
If the limit in the Prime
Number Theorem exists,

142
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:50,550
then it equals 1.

143
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,500
Why is this true?

144
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:56,850
Because if the limit is not
1, say it equals a number a,

145
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,800
it easily follows that the harmonic series

146
00:07:59,950 --> 00:08:02,750
is about a times Mertens' series,

147
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,900
contradicting Mertens' theorem.

